        "message" : [
          "%md",
          "",
          "## Findings",
          "",
          "### Finding 1: The Stolen Identity Persists on a Foreign Computer",
          "",
          "Each stolen credential was used from at most two source computers, often for weeks. The detection therefore looks for persistence, not for bursts. The fan-out happens one hop further, from the source computer to its destinations: `C17693` alone reached 296 computers. Scenario 2 analyzes that host-to-host view.",
          "",
          "### Finding 2: Compromises Hide in the Middle",
          "",
          "On event volume (215 to 573 events) and on target importance, the confirmed pairs sit in the unremarkable middle. Their PageRank scores are at least ten times lower than that of the highest-ranked unlabeled candidate. Sorting candidates by \"most events\" or \"most important computer\" points away from them. Scenario 2 shows the same pattern for host-to-host connectivity.",
          "",
          "### Finding 3: Real Signal, Huge Haystack",
          "",
          "69,648 candidate pairs match the persistence pattern, and 3 of them are confirmed. Shared computers, such as the file server `C2651` in Step 5e, carry many of these persistent relationships without any sign of compromise. The three confirmed cases also differ: only `C17693` is a major pivot point, with 296 victims. `C19932` and `C22409` stay small, contained footholds. The full picture emerges when you combine both scenarios."
        ],
